    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Brain structure and function are known to decline steadily with age, and recent models of aging implicate the circulatory environment in this process. Experiments in which young mice are exposed to aged blood demonstrate that pro-aging factors in the blood of old individuals induce hallmarks of brain aging, such as increased neuro-inflammation and a decline in neurogenesis. The inhibitory factors present in aged blood are still being identified, and whether these factors cross the highly selective blood brain barrier or act primarily through peripheral interaction with brain endothelial cells (BECs) remains unknown. In this study, we aimed to elucidate the mechanisms underlying regulation of Vascular Adhesion Molecule 1 (VCAM1), an adhesion molecule upregulated in BECs in response to aged blood and systemic inflammation, and assess whether Vcam1 deletion can rescue the decline in adult neurogenesis. We show that brain endothelial VCAM1 increases in expression in vitro and in young mice exposed to aged plasma. Additionally, while it has previously been shown that injection of aged plasma inhibits hippocampal neurogenesis in young mice, here we demonstrated that genetic deletion of Vcam1 prevents these inhibitory effects of aged plasma on hippocampal neurogenesis, suggesting that activated BECs inhibit neuronal regeneration through VCAM1-mediated peripheral interactions. Collectively, our results indicate that VCAM1 is involved in the regulation of adult neurogenesis in response to age-related systemic inflammation, making the adhesion molecule a therapeutic target for ameliorating the decline in brain function characteristic of old age.",
